<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ovra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ovra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">OVRA</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The OVRA was the secret political police of Fascist Italy, organised from 1927 under the Ministry of the Interior while Arturo Bocchini was chief of police.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/owa-obokun"><span class="enc-name">Owa Obokun</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Owa Obokun is the title of the king of Ijesha, a Yoruba kingdom in south-western Nigeria whose capital is Ilesa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/owain-cyfeiliog"><span class="enc-name">Owain Cyfeiliog</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Owain Cyfeiliog was a Welsh prince who took the southern part of Powys after the death of his uncle Madog ap Maredudd in 1160.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Owain Glyndŵr</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Owain Glyndŵr was a Welsh landowner descended from the princes of Powys who was proclaimed Prince of Wales in 1400 and led the last great revolt against English rule.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/owain-gwynedd"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/owain-gwynedd.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Owain Gwynedd</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Owain Gwynedd, son of Gruffudd ap Cynan, ruled Gwynedd from 1137 and made it the leading Welsh power.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/owain-the-bald"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/owain-the-bald.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Owain the Bald</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Owain the Bald, in Welsh Owain Foel, was the last known king of Strathclyde, the Brittonic kingdom of the Clyde valley.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/owari-province"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/owari-province.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Owari Province</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Owari Province was an old province of central Japan, corresponding to the western part of modern Aichi Prefecture around Nagoya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/owen-roe-oneill"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/owen-roe-oneill.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Owen Roe O'Neill</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Owen Roe O'Neill was an Irish soldier of the O'Neill dynasty of Ulster who served for decades in the Spanish army in Flanders.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/owina-nido"><span class="enc-name">Owina n'ido</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The owina n'ido were the hereditary guild of weavers in the Benin Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/owodo"><span class="enc-name">Owodo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Owodo was, in Edo oral tradition, the last ruler of the Ogiso dynasty of Igodomigodo, the early kingdom from which the Kingdom of Benin grew.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/owu-kingdom"><span class="enc-name">Owu Kingdom</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">Owu was a Yoruba kingdom in south-western Nigeria, one of the old states of Yorubaland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/oxford"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/oxford.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Oxford</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Oxford is a city in England, home to one of the oldest universities in Europe.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/oxfordshire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Oxfordshire</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Oxfordshire is a county in south-central England, with its county town at Oxford.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/oxib-keh"><span class="enc-name">Oxib Keh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Oxib Keh was the ajpop, or principal king, of the K'iche' kingdom in the Guatemalan highlands when the Spanish arrived.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/oxus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/oxus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Oxus</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Oxus, now called the Amu Darya, is a major river of Central Asia flowing from the Pamirs towards the Aral Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Oxyrhynchus</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Oxyrhynchus, modern el-Bahnasa, was a city of Middle Egypt west of the Nile and the capital of a nome in Ptolemaic and Roman times.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/oyake-akahachi"><span class="enc-name">Oyake Akahachi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Oyake Akahachi was a chieftain on Ishigaki Island in the Yaeyama Islands who led resistance to the Ryukyu Kingdom at the end of the fifteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/oyama-gobo"><span class="enc-name">Oyama Gobō</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Oyama Gobō was a fortified temple of the Jōdo Shinshū Honganji sect, founded in 1546 on the site of modern Kanazawa in Kaga province, Japan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/oyapock-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/oyapock-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Oyapock River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Oyapock is a river of northern South America that forms most of the border between French Guiana and the Brazilian state of Amapá, flowing about 400 km to the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/oyo-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Oyo Empire</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Oyo Empire was a Yoruba state in the savannah of present-day western Nigeria, founded according to tradition by Oranmiyan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/oyo-mesi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/oyo-mesi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Oyo Mesi</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Oyo Mesi was the council of seven hereditary chiefs, headed by the Basorun, that constrained the power of the Alaafin, the king of Oyo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/oyo-ile"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/oyo-ile.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Oyo-Ile</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Oyo-Ile, also called Old Oyo or Katunga, was the capital of the Oyo Empire, located on open savannah in present-day north-western Nigeria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/oyoko-clan"><span class="enc-name">Oyoko clan</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Oyoko clan is an Akan matrilineal clan whose leaders ruled the small state of Kumasi in the late seventeenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/oyu-tolgoi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/oyu-tolgoi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Oyu Tolgoi</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Oyu Tolgoi is a copper and gold mine in the South Gobi desert of Mongolia, one of the largest such deposits in the world.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ozdemir-pasha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ozdemir-pasha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Özdemir Pasha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Özdemir Pasha was an Ottoman commander of Circassian Mamluk origin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ozolua"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ozolua.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ozolua</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ozolua, known as Ozolua the Conqueror, was an Oba of Benin who reigned c. 1480–1504.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ozurgeti"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ozurgeti.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ozurgeti</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ozurgeti is a town in western Georgia, the administrative centre of the Guria region, a short distance inland from the Black Sea coast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/oahu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/oahu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Oʻahu</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Oʻahu is the third-largest of the Hawaiian Islands and the most populous, home to the state capital Honolulu, Waikīkī and Pearl Harbor.</span></a></li></ul>
